;;; tur/forms/effects -- algebraic effects: perform, handle, resume.
;;;
;;; An effect is an operation the code asks its caller to carry out. `defeffect`
;;; declares one (`(defeffect Ask [] : int)` -- a name, its parameters, and the
;;; type of the answer). `(perform (Ask))` raises it from anywhere below a
;;; handler, and the nearest `handle` that has a clause for `Ask` takes over: the
;;; clause receives the arguments and a continuation `k`, which is "the rest of
;;; the body, waiting for an answer". `(resume k v)` gives the answer back and the
;;; body carries on from the `perform`; a clause that never resumes ends the body
;;; early, and its value becomes the value of the whole `handle`. This is how
;;; logging, configuration, failure and generators are written without threading
;;; extra arguments. effects-system-guide has the walkthrough.
;;;
;;; Only a `perform` written directly at top level, with no handler around it, is
;;; caught by the compiler (TUR-E0008). One inside a function is not: if nothing
;;; above it handles the effect, the program aborts when it gets there
;;; (`tur: unhandled effect (tag 2)`, exit status 134, when compiled;
;;; `unhandled effect: Ask`, exit status 1, interpreted). A function may declare
;;; the effects it performs with `#fx{Ask}`; one that performs an effect missing
;;; from its declared row is TUR-E0009, and `handle` removes the effects it
;;; handles from the row.
;;;
;;; The compiled program lowers effects through a CPS backend. A function that
;;; leaves its supported subset -- one whose body uses `discontinue`, say -- is
;;; rejected with `this effect operation has no lowering here`;
;;; `TUR_TRACE_EVICT=1` names the form that caused it. The interpreter has no such
;;; limit. A few forms below are unfinished or differ between the two; the work
;;; is planned in docs/upcoming/discontinue-plan.md and
;;; docs/upcoming/effect-continuation-followups-plan.md.
;; Reference only: tools/gendocs.py reads the ;;@ lines, the compiler skips them.

;;; perform -- raise an effect and wait for the handler's answer.
;;;
;;; The argument is the effect's constructor, applied to its parameters:
;;; `(perform (Say "hi"))`. The effect must have been declared with `defeffect`
;;; (an unknown name is `perform: unknown effect`), the argument count must match
;;; (TUR-E0002) and each argument's type must match the declaration (TUR-E0001).
;;; Control passes to the nearest enclosing `handle` with a clause for the effect;
;;; if that clause calls `resume`, the `perform` evaluates to the value it passes,
;;; with the type the effect declares.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   (Effect arg ...) -- the declared effect, applied to its arguments
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The value the handler resumes with, of the effect's declared result type.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (defeffect Ask [] : int)
;;;   (println (handle (+ 1 (perform (Ask))) (Ask [] k) (resume k 41)))  ; prints 42
;;@form (perform (Effect arg ...))

;;; handle -- run an expression with handlers for the effects it performs.
;;;
;;; The first argument is the body. Each pair after it is a clause head
;;; `(Effect [param ...] k)` and the expression to run when the body performs
;;; that effect: the params bind the effect's arguments (their number must match
;;; the declaration, TUR-E0002) and `k` the continuation. A clause that ends in
;;; `(resume k v)` hands `v` back to the `perform` and the body continues; a
;;; clause that does not resume abandons the rest of the body, and its value is the
;;; value of the `handle`. If the body finishes without performing, the `handle`
;;; has the body's value -- there is no separate return clause. Every clause body
;;; must have the type of the whole `handle` (TUR-E0252), or not return at all.
;;; A clause for an effect the body never performs is the warning TUR-W0033.
;;;
;;; The handler is deep: it stays in place for the whole of the continuation, so
;;; one `handle` answers every `perform` of that effect in the body. Clause bodies
;;; run outside it -- a clause that performs an effect reaches the next `handle`
;;; out, which can be a clause of an outer handler -- and `resume` may be called
;;; in a non-tail position: `(* 2 (resume k 10))` doubles whatever the rest of the
;;; body produced. Resuming `k` twice is TUR-E0201 unless it was marked
;;; `^multishot`; `^linear` makes resuming it exactly once a requirement
;;; (TUR-E0100 when a path does not, TUR-E0101 for a second use). See `resume`.
;;;
;;; Defers in the body run when the body is abandoned or finishes. When a clause
;;; does not resume, the compiled program runs them as the `handle` returns; the
;;; interpreter runs them later, at the end of an enclosing scope
;;; (docs/reported/turi-fiber-handle-defers-fire-at-the-wrong-time.md).
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   body   -- the expression to run
;;;   clause -- an `(Effect [param ...] k)` head followed by its handler expression,
;;;             repeated; the head may be `(Effect [param ...] ^multishot k)`
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The body's value, or the value of the clause that took over.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (defeffect Ask [] : int)
;;;   (defeffect Log [msg : cstr] : nil)
;;;   (println (handle (do (perform (Log "a")) (+ (perform (Ask)) (perform (Ask))))
;;;              (Ask [] k) (resume k 5)
;;;              (Log [m] k) (do (println m) (resume k nil))))  ; prints a, then 10
;;@form (handle body (Effect [param ...] k) handler-expr ...)

;;; handle-shallow -- like handle, but the handler answers only one perform.
;;;
;;; A shallow handler is taken down after it has run once: the continuation `k` a
;;; clause resumes runs without it, so the next `perform` of that effect reaches
;;; an enclosing handler (or aborts as unhandled if there is none). Where a deep
;;; `handle` is one fixed answer for the whole body, this lets a clause choose
;;; what handles the rest, by wrapping the `resume` in another `handle`. The syntax
;;; and the typing rules are those of `handle`. Because the body can still
;;; perform the effect after the handler has gone, `handle-shallow` does not
;;; remove the effect from the enclosing function's declared row: a function
;;; declared `#fx{}` that handles only shallowly is TUR-E0009. At the top level
;;; of a file a `handle-shallow` statement works like a `handle` one.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   body   -- the expression to run
;;;   clause -- an `(Effect [param ...] k)` head followed by its handler expression,
;;;             repeated
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The body's value, or the value of the clause that took over.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (defeffect Op [] : int)
;;;   (defn two [] #fx{Op} : int (+ (perform (Op)) (perform (Op))))
;;;   (defn main [] : int
;;;     (println (handle (handle-shallow (two) (Op [] k) (resume k 5))
;;;                (Op [] k) (resume k 100)))  ; prints 105: the second perform goes outward
;;;     0)
;;@form (handle-shallow body (Effect [param ...] k) handler-expr ...)

;;; resume -- continue the body from the perform with a value.
;;;
;;; Valid only on the continuation a handler clause binds (`k` in
;;; `(Effect [p] k)`); a literal is TUR-E0001, and a continuation made by
;;; `shift`, `call/cc` or the cloneable and serial variants is called instead,
;;; `(k v)`. `(k v)` on a handler continuation is the same as `(resume k v)`. The
;;; value becomes the result of the `perform`. The `resume` expression itself
;;; yields what the rest of the body, and so the `handle`, produces -- not the
;;; value passed in -- which is why a clause can do more work after it:
;;; `(+ 100 (resume k 1))`.
;;;
;;; A continuation is one-shot: resuming it twice is TUR-E0201. Mark it
;;; `^multishot` -- `(Flip [] ^multishot k)` -- to resume it any number of times,
;;; each running the rest of the body afresh; `(+ (resume k 10) (resume k 20))`
;;; explores two answers (the standard way to write search and backtracking).
;;; `^linear` demands exactly one resume or `discontinue` on every path. A
;;; `^multishot` continuation cannot be resumed inside `atomically` (TUR-E0502).
;;; The value must be of the effect's declared result type: `(resume k "x")` for
;;; an effect that answers `int` is TUR-E0001. (Only the scalar types are
;;; compared: integers, floats, bool and cstr; a `nil`-result effect takes any
;;; filler, as in `(resume k 0)`.)
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   k     -- the continuation bound by the handler clause
;;;   value -- the value the `perform` evaluates to
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The value the rest of the body produces.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (defeffect Ask [] : int)
;;;   (println (handle (+ 1 (perform (Ask))) (Ask [] k) (* 2 (resume k 10))))  ; prints 22
;;;   (println (handle (+ 1 (perform (Ask)))
;;;              (Ask [] ^multishot k) (+ (resume k 10) (resume k 20))))  ; prints 32
;;@form (resume k value)

;;; discontinue -- abort the body from a handler clause with an exception.
;;;
;;; Meant to resume the continuation by raising a failure at the `perform`
;;; instead of returning a value, so that the body's cleanup runs. It is typed
;;; `nil`, so a `handle` whose clause is a bare `discontinue` must itself be of
;;; type `nil` (TUR-E0252 otherwise). Not usable today: the compiled program
;;; rejects any function that contains it (a located error naming `discontinue`,
;;; beside the `no lowering here` one on the `perform`), and the interpreter
;;; stops with `discontinue is not supported`. Implementing it is planned
;;; (docs/upcoming/discontinue-plan.md): the `perform` will raise a panic in the
;;; body, which a `catch-unwind` there can catch. Until then, to end the body
;;; early leave the clause without resuming it (the body's `defer`s still run);
;;; for a failure the caller sees, return a `Result` from the clause. Like
;;; `resume`, it consumes the continuation, so
;;; using `k` afterwards is TUR-E0201 (TUR-E0101 for a `^linear` one), and it
;;; satisfies the `^linear` requirement.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   k         -- the continuation bound by the handler clause
;;;   exception -- the failure to raise at the `perform`, normally a string
;;@form (discontinue k exception)

;;; try-with -- another spelling of handle.
;;;
;;; Exactly `handle`: the body, then `(Effect [param ...] k)` clauses each followed
;;; by its expression, with the same typing, `resume` and `^multishot` rules. The
;;; name is there for readers who know it from OCaml, and for async code, where
;;; `(try-with (await (fetch-file path)) (FileError [p] k) (resume k default))` reads as an
;;; exception handler (async-await-guide).
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   body   -- the expression to run
;;;   clause -- an `(Effect [param ...] k)` head followed by its handler expression,
;;;             repeated
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The body's value, or the value of the clause that took over.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (defeffect Ask [] : int)
;;;   (defn main [] : int
;;;     (println (try-with (+ 1 (perform (Ask))) (Ask [] k) (resume k 41)))  ; prints 42
;;;     0)
;;@form (try-with body (Effect [param ...] k) handler-expr ...)

;;; with-handler -- run a body under a handler value.
;;;
;;; With exactly two arguments, the first is a handler value and the second the
;;; body: `(with-handler h body)`. A handler value is built by `(handler (Effect
;;; [param ...] k) expr)`, by `compose-handlers`, or is a variable or parameter
;;; holding one, so a handler can be defined once and applied to many bodies or
;;; passed to a function. Its type is `(handler Effect Param Result)`: the effect,
;;; the type of its first parameter (`int` when it has none) and the type its
;;; clause produces. A non-handler first argument is `first argument must be a
;;; handler value`. With any other number of arguments it is `handle`, body first.
;;;
;;; `(with-handler (handler ...) body)` with a literal handler removes the effect
;;; from the enclosing function's row like `handle`; a handler held in a variable
;;; removes nothing, so a function declared `#fx{}` that applies a variable
;;; handler is TUR-E0009.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   handler -- a handler value
;;;   body    -- the expression to run under it
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The body's value, or the value of the clause that took over.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (defeffect Ask [] : int)
;;;   (defn ask-plus [n : int] : int (+ n (perform (Ask))))
;;;   (defn main [] : int
;;;     (let [h (handler (Ask [] k) (resume k 41))]
;;;       (println (with-handler h (ask-plus 1))))  ; prints 42
;;;     0)
;;@form (with-handler handler body)
;;@form (with-handler body (Effect [param ...] k) handler-expr ...)

;;; compose-handlers -- combine two handler values into one.
;;;
;;; `(compose-handlers h1 h2)` is a handler that handles what either does; both
;;; arguments must be handler values (`first argument must be a handler value`
;;; otherwise). They must handle different effects: an effect in both is;;; TUR-E0251. `(with-handler (compose-handlers h1 h2) body)` means
;;; `(with-handler h1 (with-handler h2 body))`, h1 outermost (first-class-handlers-
;;; semantics): a clause of h2 may perform an effect h1 answers, not the other
;;; way round. That is how it runs when the composed arguments are `handler`
;;; literals (written in place, nested to any depth). A composed handler built
;;; earlier and held in a variable is still one flat table, where a clause that
;;; performs an effect of its sibling aborts as unhandled; nest `with-handler`s
;;; for that.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   h1 -- a handler value
;;;   h2 -- a handler value for different effects
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   A handler value to pass to `with-handler`.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (defeffect Ask [] : int)
;;;   (defeffect Log [msg : cstr] : nil)
;;;   (defn both [] : int
;;;     (with-handler (compose-handlers (handler (Ask [] k) (resume k 41))
;;;                                     (handler (Log [m] k) (do (println m) (resume k nil))))
;;;       (do (perform (Log "hi")) (+ 1 (perform (Ask))))))
;;;   (println (both))  ; prints hi, then 42
;;@form (compose-handlers h1 h2)

;;; cont? -- can this continuation still be invoked?
;;;
;;; True for a continuation that has not been used up: a handler clause's `k`
;;; before it is resumed, an escape continuation while its `call/cc` is still on
;;; the stack, and a cloneable or serial continuation always, since those can be
;;; invoked repeatedly. Anything that is not a continuation -- `5` -- gives false.
;;; A handler's `k` is false once it has been resumed, on both engines. That
;;; includes a `^multishot` one compiled, so it cannot guard a second `resume`
;;; there; interpreted, a `^multishot` clause that calls `cont?` between two
;;; `resume`s fails the second with `this continuation has already been
;;; resumed`. Use it before the first use.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   k -- a continuation, or any value
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   `true` if `k` can still be invoked, else `false`.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (cont? 5)  ; => false
;;;   (defeffect Ask [] : int)
;;;   (println (handle (+ 1 (perform (Ask))) (Ask [] k) (if (cont? k) (resume k 41) 0)))  ; prints 42
;;@form (cont? k)
